---
// src/pages/blue-staffy-health-uk/index.astro — REBUILT from the approved board record
// (data/boards/blue-staffy-health-uk.json, approved 2026-09-20). Hand-written and NOT
// regenerated by `npm run extract`: the slug is listed in data/facts/rebuilt.json, so
// `migration_parity.py` skips it and `facts_preserved_check.py`, `link_parity_check.py` and
// `verbatim_set_check.py` are its gates instead.
//
// THIS IS THE HEALTH PAGE, AND IT OWNS THE DNA-TEST FACTS. The two tests and the two parents'
// status live in this record's `dna-tests` table and nowhere else: /blue-staffy-uk-breeders/
// and the listing both COUNT that table (`#len(sections[dna-tests].table.rows)`) for their own
// counter tiles, so the row count and the two ids are a contract, not a layout choice. There
// are TWO tests, L-2-HGA and HC-HSF4, and there is no third — the breed has screens for two,
// the migrated body names two, and a third appearing here would be a credential nobody can
// check (rule 9).
//
// THE STRATEGY IS "THE EVIDENCE TABLE" (`brief.strategy`). Of the four breeder pages scanned,
// three explain the two DNA tests well and not one states a result for its own dogs. Ours name
// the tests and state no result either (answer board q01, 2026-09-29: the breeder holds no DNA
// certificates), so the table goes high and the ordinary care that keeps a Staffy well follows it.
//
// WHAT THIS PAGE MAY NOT SAY, and none of it is an oversight (`brief.out_of_scope`):
// no hand-typed guarantee length (it is data/settings.json `guarantee_label`, read through
// guaranteeLabel(); answer board q07, 2026-09-29), no
// £850 to £1,200 band (below the locked prices, Known Issue 11, `dropped.prices`), no former
// city and no licence wording (Known Issue 16 / LICENCE_CLAIM_PLACEHOLDER), no named testing
// laboratory, no prevalence figure, percentage or prognosis for any of the five conditions,
// no newsletter block and no subscriber count, and no enquiry form — the contact page carries
// the one form the form audit measures and this page links to it.
//
// NO EMBED. `data/facts/blue-staffy-health-uk.json` records `embeds: []` and `brief.scope`
// says so in as many words, so working rule 14 asks this page for nothing: there is no id to
// carry, and minting one would be the thing that rule forbids. All EIGHT recorded images are
// carried, at their original paths with their original alt text (working rule 11).
//
// WORKING RULE 15 (faithful rewrite). The VERBATIM SET is carried WORD FOR WORD and is READ
// OFF THE RECORD (`sections[].heading`, `sections[].verbatim_opening`, the tree nodes' own
// pair, `assets[].alt`, and the `data/faq.json` rows the record names) rather than retyped
// here, so a curly quote cannot drift between the record the breeder approved and the page
// that ships. Twenty-one deviations are recorded in `verbatim.changed` with their reasons.
// THE H1 IS NOT ONE OF THEM: `h1.pick` is variant 0, which IS the migrated H1 word for word,
// so no `h1` row is owed and none is written. The slug's keyword phrase sits in the migrated
// H2 this hero carries ("Blue Staffy Health UK : Our Commitment to Happy, Healthy Puppies").
//
// WORKING RULE 16. The hero is `H-GD1` — the editorial SPLIT: the copy on the left, the
// photograph on the right, and the key-facts aside as a second row under the copy — and the
// counter is `C-GD1`, ruled columns beside the heading. Both are the interior-guide family's
// own arrangements, resolved through `pickedStyle()`, and neither is one any other page took:
// the three guides were re-boarded onto three different heroes (Known Issues 30 and 35).
//
// THE ASIDE IS THE RECORD'S OWN `hero.aside`, AND EVERY ROW OF IT IS DERIVED (amendment 10.5,
// 10.6). "L-2-HGA and HC-HSF4" is built from the table's own row labels, "DNA tested" from
// its own status column, and the lifespan from the BREED GUIDE's cell — this page cites that
// cell and does not retype it. Each derived value is asserted against the wording the breeder
// approved, so a fact that moves throws at build with both readings in the message rather than
// printing one nobody has checked. Each row is found by the PATH IT CITES rather than by its
// label, so a row renamed on the board cannot pick up another row's derivation. The three
// approved CHIPS are rendered in the same box as a single credential line, except on H-GD1,
// where the band has no room for the line (see the aside below; Known Issue 35): `Hero` shows
// a chip ROW only for `ledge: 'chips'`, so on H-GD3 the prop is handed over and dropped, and a claim
// the breeder approved is not worth losing to an arrangement — the chips are on this page, in
// the aside, and it is only the component's own chip row that H-GD3 does not render.
//
// THE CONTENTS THE STYLE'S NAME PROMISES IS `PageNav`, THREE LINES BELOW THE HERO, and that is
// a measurement rather than a preference. The aside is a 15rem column inside a band held
// between 390 and 450px at 1280 (rules/design.md rule 10); this record has FIFTEEN sections,
// and a fifteen-row contents list in that column is taller than the whole band before the key
// facts are added to it. `PageShell` mounts `PageNav` from `sectionsFromRecord(record)` — the
// same ids the `<section>` elements carry and the same labels — so the real contents are one
// glance below, derived from the record rather than typed.
//
// THREE FIGURES, ALL COUNTED AT BUILD (amendment 10.5, and the lesson da9c34e wrote down).
// The record's `n` is a hand-written number — true on the day it was boarded — so each tile is
// derived from the file its `source` names and a reading that no longer matches what the
// breeder approved throws with both numbers in the message.
//
// NINE SECTIONS CARRY NO PICK. `screening`, `vaccinations`, `registration`, `vet-checks`,
// `diet`, `exercise`, `conditions`, `lifespan` and `talk-to-us` have `options.pick: null`, so
// no arrangement was ever chosen for them and none is invented: they render with no `bl-box`
// class at all, exactly as the about page's six no-pick sections do.
//
// SCHEMA IS FAQPage AND NOTHING ELSE MINTED HERE. `brief.schema.types` is WebPage +
// Organization + FAQPage: BaseLayout already emits the `#webpage` node with the git-derived
// dates and `Schema.astro` already emits the sitewide `#business` LocalBusiness (which IS an
// Organization) from data/settings.json, at TOWN level with no geo (Known Issue 16). The
// record asks for no MedicalWebPage, so none is written — a health page that claimed the
// medical vocabulary would be asserting a standing this site does not have.
//
// H4-H6 ARE WRITTEN HERE, not in the record (amendment 2.1): the approved outline stops at the
// H3 the breeder read. Every ladder sentence traces to the migrated body or to a file under
// data/, and the H6 lines all open on the record's own `tuple.h6_prefixes` entry,
// "Health Note:". Every heading renders through `titleCase()` (amendment 4b).
import PageShell from '../../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/kit/Hero.astro';
import CounterStrip from '../../components/kit/CounterStrip.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/kit/DataTable.astro';
import InfoCard from '../../components/kit/InfoCard.astro';
import Testimonial from '../../components/kit/Testimonial.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/kit/Faq.astro';
import { sectionsFromRecord } from '../../lib/sections';
import { pickedStyle, approvalInForce } from '../../lib/pickedStyle';
import { bakedAssets, bakedSrcset, inPublic, BODY_SIZES } from '../../lib/assets';
import { recordText, countedFigures, ledgeText, sourceKey, breedFactsCell,
  type LedgeEntry } from '../../lib/recordText';
import { boxClass } from '../../lib/boardStyles';
import { titleCase as T } from '../../lib/headings';
import { loadFaq } from '../../lib/faq';
import { SITE, abs, guaranteeLabel, guaranteeCoverSentence } from '../../lib/site';
import reviewsJson from '../../../data/reviews.json';
import settings from '../../../data/settings.json';
import prices from '../../../data/price-matrix.json';
import guideRecord from '../../../data/boards/uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide.json';
import record from '../../../data/boards/blue-staffy-health-uk.json';

const path = '/blue-staffy-health-uk/';
// The picked meta variants and H1, BY INDEX from the approval IN FORCE — never retyped here.
// `approvalInForce()`, not `record.approval`: the record went back to the board for its hero
// (Known Issue 35) with its approval carried under `approval_previous`, and what the page is
// called was never the question (spec §9 amendment 11a).
const approved = approvalInForce(record);
const title = record.meta_set.titles[approved.meta!.title];
const description = record.meta_set.descriptions[approved.meta!.description];
const h1 = record.h1.variants[approved.h1];

// The eight migrated photographs, by slot, at their ORIGINAL public paths with the alt text
// the record baked (working rule 11; all eight alts are elements of the verbatim set). A
// missing slot throws at build.
const asset = bakedAssets(record);
const litter = asset('health-litter');
const testsGraphic = asset('health-tests-graphic');
const maggie = asset('health-maggie-dam');
const vaccinationPhoto = asset('health-vaccinations');
const vetCheck = asset('health-vet-check');
const feeding = asset('health-feeding');
const certifiedBreeders = asset('health-certified-breeders');
const kcPapers = asset('health-kc-papers');

// The carried wording, read off the record rather than retyped (see the rule 15 note above).
const { sec, heading, opening, node, nodeOpening } = recordText(record);

// THE REVIEW, BY NAME rather than by index. The record's intent states MODE: single on
// data/reviews.json entry 2 — Mark J, London — inside `record_hash`: he is the only one of the
// three who names the paperwork and the health testing, which is exactly what every section
// above him claims. WHICH review is the record's sentence; HOW the block is drawn is the pick.
interface Review { quote: string; name: string; place: string; source: string }
const markJ = (reviewsJson as Review[]).filter((r) => r.name === 'Mark J');
if (markJ.length !== 1) {
  throw new Error('blue-staffy-health-uk: data/reviews.json no longer holds the Mark J row');
}

// THE NINE FAQ ROWS THE RECORD NAMES, BY ID, in the record's order. Eight of the nine carry the
// migrated accordion's own questions word for word (rule 15); `puppy-package` is the outline's.
// The ninth migrated question — the guarantee's length — is a `verbatim.changed` drop, recorded
// when no length was held; the section's intro and H5 now answer it from data/settings.json
// `guarantee_label` (answer board q07, 2026-09-29). The answers are data, not page copy.
const faqSection = record.sections.find((s) => s.id === 'questions')!;
const faqRows = loadFaq();
const faqs = faqSection.tree.map((n) => {
  const row = faqRows.find((r) => r.id === n.heading);
  if (!row) throw new Error(`blue-staffy-health-uk: data/faq.json has no row ${n.heading}`);
  return row;
});

// ── THE FACTS THIS PAGE OWNS, AND THE ONE IT CITES ──────────────────────────────────────────
// The DNA table is the source every other page's counter reads. Its ROW COUNT and its two test
// ids are read here rather than typed, so the number this page prints and the number the about
// page prints can never be two different hand-written twos.
const dnaTable = sec('dna-tests').table!;
const DNA_ROWS = 'data/boards/blue-staffy-health-uk.json#len(sections[dna-tests].table.rows)';
const testNames = dnaTable.rows.map((r) => String(r[0]));
if (testNames.length !== 2) {
  throw new Error(`blue-staffy-health-uk: the DNA table holds ${testNames.length} tests and this `
    + 'page, the about page and the listing are all written on there being two');
}
// "DNA tested" in the hero aside is the table's own status column, taken once: every row must
// carry the same status, and it names the test, never a result. The breeder holds no DNA
// certificates for Maggie and Jones (answer board q01, 2026-09-29), so a status that states a
// result ("clear", "negative", "carrier", "affected") stops the build rather than printing.
const statuses = dnaTable.rows.map((r) => String(r[2]));
const statusSet = new Set(statuses);
if (statusSet.size !== 1 || /\b(?:clear|results?|negative|carriers?|affected)\b/i.test(statuses[0])) {
  throw new Error(`blue-staffy-health-uk: the DNA rows read ${statuses.join(' / ')} — the hero aside `
    + 'prints one status for both parents, and it names the test only (answer board q01)');
}
const [parentsStatus] = [...statusSet];

// THE LIFESPAN IS THE BREED GUIDE'S CELL, CITED AND NOT RETYPED. Both the hero aside and the
// third counter tile stand on `sections[breed-facts].table.rows` → "Lifespan" in
// data/boards/uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide.json. The tile prints the range and the aside
// prints the clause, and both are cut from that sentence here — so the day the guide's cell
// moves, this page stops building rather than quietly disagreeing with the page it belongs to.
// ONE PARSER, TWO CALLERS: `breedFactsCell()` is the lookup and the cut, and the breed guide —
// which OWNS the table — reads its own three cells through the same function. Two copies of one
// reading is the thing a citation exists to prevent.
const LIFESPAN_SOURCE = 'data/boards/uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide.json#cell(sections[breed-facts].table.rows, Lifespan)';
const lifespan = breedFactsCell(guideRecord, 'Lifespan', 'blue-staffy-health-uk');
const lifespanCell = lifespan.text;
const lifespanRange = lifespan.range();
const lifespanClause = lifespan.clause;

const COUNTED: Record<string, string> = {
  [DNA_ROWS]: String(dnaTable.rows.length),
  [LIFESPAN_SOURCE]: lifespanRange,
};
const counted = countedFigures(COUNTED, 'blue-staffy-health-uk');
const counterStats = sec('stats').stats!.map(counted);

// ── THE HERO'S OWN DATA ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const heroSection = sec('top');
const heroChips = (heroSection.hero!.chips as LedgeEntry[]).map(ledgeText);
const heroAside = heroSection.hero!.aside!;
// EVERY ASIDE ROW IS DERIVED, AND IT IS FOUND BY THE PATH IT CITES RATHER THAN BY ITS LABEL.
// All three rows now carry a `source` (added in the 2026-09-21 re-approval), so the row that
// states the two test names is the row citing the two label cells, wherever it sits and
// whatever it is called — a row renamed or moved on the board cannot silently pick up another
// row's derivation, and a row citing a path this page cannot rebuild throws rather than
// printing wording off the record. `sourceKey()` is the same join `countedFigures` uses, so a
// value standing on TWO cells is keyed on both of them (amendment 10.5).
const DNA_NAMES = dnaTable.rows.map((_, i) =>
  `data/boards/blue-staffy-health-uk.json#sections[dna-tests].table.rows[${i}][0]`);
const DNA_STATUSES = testNames.map((t) =>
  `data/boards/blue-staffy-health-uk.json#cell(sections[dna-tests].table.rows, ${t})`);
const ASIDE_VALUES: Record<string, string> = {
  [sourceKey(DNA_NAMES)]: testNames.join(' and '),
  [sourceKey(DNA_STATUSES)]: parentsStatus,
  [sourceKey(LIFESPAN_SOURCE)]: lifespanClause,
};
const asideRows = heroAside.rows!.map((r) => {
  const key = sourceKey((r as { source?: string | string[] }).source);
  const derived = ASIDE_VALUES[key];
  if (derived === undefined) {
    throw new Error(`blue-staffy-health-uk: the hero aside row "${r.label}" cites `
      + `${key || '(no source)'}, which this page has no build-time derivation for — an aside `
      + 'row states a fact and rule 9 gives it no other source');
  }
  if (derived !== r.value) {
    throw new Error(`blue-staffy-health-uk: the aside row "${r.label}" reads "${derived}" today `
      + `and the approved record expects "${r.value}" — the fact moved, so the board needs a look`);
  }
  return { label: r.label, value: derived };
});

// THE CANDIDATE LISTS ARE BUILT FROM THE FILES THAT EXIST, not spelled into the markup (the fix
// 1c500e5 made): a hand-written `srcset` is a filename duplicated away from its file, and a 404
// candidate is invisible to `img-srcset-within-2x` because the master is what paints.
const srcsetOf = (a: ReturnType<typeof asset>, widths: number[]) => bakedSrcset(a, widths, inPublic);
// The panel hero's photo column is 14rem at 1024 and above and the full width of a phone below
// 900px, so two small candidates are baked beside the master rather than the master being
// re-encoded (working rule 11); `Hero` owns the `sizes` attribute that chooses between them.
//
// IN THE BODY, `sizes` IS WRITTEN ONLY WHERE THERE ARE CANDIDATES TO CHOOSE BETWEEN. Five of
// this page's eight photographs are 500-550px masters against a 420px column — inside
// `img-srcset-within-2x` at every width, so nothing is baked beside them — and a `sizes` on an
// image with no `srcset` is inert markup that reads like a decision. It is on the two that DO
// offer candidates, the 780px dam and the 1080px vet check, and on nowhere else.

const sections = sectionsFromRecord(record);

// Each section's approved arrangement, resolved through the same `boxClass()` the board preview
// called: what the breeder signed off is what ships. Only the six sections that carry a pick are
// ever asked for one.
const style = (id: string) => pickedStyle(record, id);
const box = (id: string) => boxClass(style(id));
const heroStyle = style('top');
const counterStyle = style('stats');
const reviewStyle = style('paperwork-review');

const town = SITE.location_label.split(' · ')[0];
const hours = SITE.hours.replace('Mo-Su ', 'from ').replace('-', ' to ');
const deposit = `£${settings.deposit_gbp}, ${settings.deposit_refundable ? 'refundable' : 'non-refundable'}`;

// FAQPage, from the SAME nine rows the accordion renders. data/faq.json keeps the sentence-case
// wording, which is what `name` reads (amendment 6g).
const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': `${abs(path)}#faq`,
    mainEntity: faqs.map((r) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: r.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: r.a },
    })),
  },
];
---
<PageShell
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={path}
  ogType="article"
  schema={pageSchema}
  sections={sections}
>
  {/* 01 top — hero, picked style H-GD1 (the editorial split: the copy left, the photograph
      right, the key-facts aside as a second row under the copy — Known Issue 35). The aside
      is a SLOT (amendment 8) and what goes in it is the record's own `hero.aside`, every row of
      it derived above; on H-GD1 its three chips are left out of that box (the credential line
      the band has no room for, below), and any other arrangement prints them at its foot. No
      CTA buttons: the record gives the hero none (amendment 6a), and no separate chip ROW,
      because the arrangement's ledge is `aside`. */}
  <section slot="hero" id="top" data-section-label="Blue Staffy health" class={box('top')}>
    <Hero
      as="h1"
      eyebrow={`Health testing and care · ${SITE.location_label}`}
      title={T(h1)}
      {/* ONE SENTENCE. The desktop lede is `-webkit-line-clamp: 2` and a clamp HIDES its
          overflow, so a third line disappears rather than pushing anything past the ceiling —
          invisible on the page, and only a measurement finds it. */}
      lede="Two DNA tests, two parents, and the paperwork behind every puppy that leaves here."
      image={litter.file}
      imageAlt={litter.alt}
      imageWidth={litter.w}
      imageHeight={litter.h}
      imageSrcset={srcsetOf(litter, [240, 400])}
      layout={heroStyle.layout.hero ?? 'panel'}
      align={heroStyle.layout.align ?? 'left'}
      media={heroStyle.layout.media ?? 'left'}
      ledge={heroStyle.layout.ledge ?? 'aside'}
      chips={heroChips}
    >
      {/* THE ASIDE IS THE PAGE'S MARKUP, and it carries the record's chips as well as its rows.
          `Hero` renders the chip row only for `ledge: 'chips'` — correctly, because a hero
          showing two ledges is neither arrangement — so on H-GD3 the prop is handed over and
          dropped. The three chips are rendered here as a single credential line rather than a
          wrapped row, because a row costs the card two lines it does not have. */}
      <div slot="aside">
        <p class="aside-title">{heroAside.title}</p>
        <dl class="aside-rows">
          {asideRows.map((r) => (<><dt>{r.label}</dt><dd>{r.value}</dd></>))}
        </dl>
        {/* On H-GD1, the editorial split, the aside is a second row under the copy and the
            credential line is the one line the 450px band has no room for (measured at 1280:
            30px over with it, 0 without; Known Issue 35). The chips are still passed to `Hero`
            above, so any other arrangement prints the line again. */}
        {heroStyle.layout.hero !== 'split' && <p class="aside-chips">{heroChips.join(' · ')}</p>}
      </div>
    </Hero>
    <h2>{T(heading('top'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('top')}</p>
    <p>
      What follows is the working rather than the promise: the two conditions a Staffordshire
      Bull Terrier is screened for and where Maggie and Jones stand on each, then the care that
      decides where in a lifespan a dog lands. Where a fact is not established it is absent —
      no laboratory named, no percentage anywhere.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* 02 takeaways — takeaways, picked style S2 (three cards across on the steel band, the
      heading as an eyebrow). FOUR cards, which is what the record's tree carries, so the
      auto-fit grid wraps to 3+1. Each card is the one-line form of a section below it and
      nothing here is new: the two DNA tests, the microchip, the health card and the lifespan
      range are all stated at length further down, and all four come off a file. */}
  <section id="takeaways" data-section-label="What a health-tested litter means" class={box('takeaways')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <p class="bl-eyebrow">The summary, before the working</p>
      <h2>{T(heading('takeaways'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">
        Four lines, each with a file behind it rather than a badge.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <div class="bl-cards">
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 0).heading)}
          body={`We DNA test Maggie and Jones for ${testNames.join(' and ')}, the pair of inherited conditions the breed is screened for.`}
        />
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 1).heading)}
          body="Every puppy is chipped before it goes, as the law has required since 2016, and the details are ready to transfer into your name."
        />
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 2).heading)}
          body="The first vaccination is done here, and the card travelling with the puppy records it beside the chip number and the vet check."
        />
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 3).heading)}
          body={`The Staffordshire Bull Terrier Club's figure for a well-cared-for dog is ${lifespanCell}.`}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 03 stats — the counter strip, picked style C-GD1 (ruled columns beside the heading, each
      label under its figure): the interior-guide family's quietest counter, which is the right
      one for a page whose figures are a count and a range rather than a price. Both are
      DERIVED at build from the files their `source` names and checked against the number the
      breeder approved — the row count off this page's own table and the range off the breed
      guide's cell. (A third tile, the parents' DNA status, came out on 2026-09-29: it stated a
      result, answer board q01.) */}
  <section id="stats" data-section-label="The health figures" class={box('stats')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('stats'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">
        A count and a range, both read out of the records below.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <CounterStrip
        stats={counterStats}
        tiles={counterStyle.layout.tiles ?? 'ruled'}
        label={counterStyle.layout.label ?? 'under'}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 04 dna-tests — table, picked style S2 (zebra rows inside a card). THE TABLE THIS PAGE
      OWNS. Three columns and two rows, every status taken from the migrated body's own
      sentence; no laboratory is named, because no file on disk names one. It stacks into
      labelled rows below 640px — `DataTable` carries `.stack-table` unconditionally (working
      rule 13) — and the two registry links are the pages for the two tests themselves rather
      than the registry's DNA-testing shop, which `dropped.links` strikes. */}
  <section id="dna-tests" data-section-label="The two DNA tests" class={box('dna-tests')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('dna-tests'))}</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <p>{opening('dna-tests')}</p>
      <DataTable
        caption={dnaTable.caption}
        columns={dnaTable.columns}
        rows={dnaTable.rows}
      />
      <img
        class="bl-img"
        src={testsGraphic.file}
        alt={testsGraphic.alt}
        width={testsGraphic.w}
        height={testsGraphic.h}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <h3>{T(node('dna-tests', 0).heading)}</h3>
      <p>
        Both conditions are recessive: a puppy needs two copies to be affected, one from each
        parent, which is why both parents are tested. A DNA test speaks to these two conditions
        and to nothing else.
        <a href="/uk-blue-staffy-breeders-contact/">Ask us about the two tests</a> before
        you travel.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="https://www.royalkennelclub.com/health-and-dog-care/health-dog-care/health/getting-started-with-health-testing-and-screening/dna-testing/dna-test-l-2hga/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the registry's own page on the L-2-HGA DNA test</a> and
        <a href="https://www.royalkennelclub.com/health-and-dog-care/health-dog-care/health/getting-started-with-health-testing-and-screening/dna-testing/dna-test-hc-hsf4/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the registry's own page on the HC-HSF4 DNA test</a> say
        what each screen looks for.
      </p>
      <h4>{T('Two Tests, And There Is No Third')}</h4>
      <p>
        Two is the number of DNA screens the breed has, so two is the number this page prints.
        A further test, appearing on our own site and in none of our files, would be worth
        nothing to you and would make the two that are real harder to trust.
      </p>
      <h5>{T('A Test Belongs To A Dog, Not To A Kennel')}</h5>
      <p>
        The table names Maggie and Jones because a DNA test is taken by one dog. A breeder who
        reports testing as a house policy is reporting something no one can check against a dog.
      </p>
      <h6>{T('Health Note: Ask which tests, not just whether')}</h6>
      <p>Ask any breeder which tests each parent has had, and ask to see the vet records behind the rest.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 05 screening — standard, and NO PICK: `options.pick` is null on this record, so no
      arrangement was ever chosen for it and none is invented here. The H2 and the opening are
      migrated wording, as are two of the four H3s and their own openings. Beyond the DNA tests:
      the BVA/KC eye examination and patella grading, both stated as what we DO rather than as a
      score, because no file on disk holds a grade. No accreditation is claimed (Known Issue 7). */}
  <section id="screening" data-section-label="The wider screening">
    <h2>{T(heading('screening'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('screening')}</p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={maggie.file}
      alt={maggie.alt}
      width={maggie.w}
      height={maggie.h}
      {/* 780px master, 2.08x on a phone — a 400 candidate is baked BESIDE it (working rule 11:
          the served file is untouched) and `sizes` says what the column actually paints. */}
      srcset={srcsetOf(maggie, [400, 760])}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h3>{T(node('screening', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('screening', 0)}</p>
    <p>
      The HC-HSF4 test covers one inherited cataract and one only; an examination by a vet on
      the scheme looks at the eye itself for what the DNA test cannot see.
      <a href="https://www.bva.co.uk/canine-health-schemes/eye-scheme/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the BVA's own description of the eye scheme</a> sets
      out who may carry one out.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('screening', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Knee health is assessed before any mating. We state that we do it and print no grade,
      because a grade is a number and no file we keep holds one.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('screening', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('screening', 2)}</p>

    <h3>{T(node('screening', 3).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Because a recessive condition is decided before a puppy exists. Once a litter is born,
      screening only reports; before a mating it can still change the outcome, which is why the
      tests here are the parents'.
      <a href="/blue-staffy-uk-breeders/">Read about the two parents and the home they live in</a> if
      you would rather meet the dogs behind the tests first.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('No Scheme, No Accreditation, No Body Named')}</h4>
    <p>
      No scheme membership, no accreditation and no standards body. None is recorded in any file
      we keep, and a body named without a document behind it is a borrowed reputation.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('Screened Before The Mating, Not After It')}</h5>
    <p>
      Every screen here happens before a pairing is agreed, which makes it a decision rather
      than a record.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Health Note: Ask what was examined and when')}</h6>
    <p>An examination has a date on it, and the date is the part that says whether it is current.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 06 vaccinations — standard, NO PICK. The most-asked question the migrated body records,
      and the one with a legal answer attached: the chip is a requirement a buyer can be
      prosecuted over. The first, second and fifth H3s carry migrated wording and their own
      openings; the second dose and the booster are named as the OWNER'S vet's, because they
      are. No kennel-cough row: the migrated body calls it optional and it is the owner's vet's
      decision (`dropped.text`). No turnaround window is promised anywhere. */}
  <section id="vaccinations" data-section-label="Vaccinations and the health card">
    <h2>{T(heading('vaccinations'))}</h2>
    <p>
      A puppy leaving here has had the first part of a course somebody else finishes: what is
      already done, then what your own vet does next.
    </p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={vaccinationPhoto.file}
      alt={vaccinationPhoto.alt}
      width={vaccinationPhoto.w}
      height={vaccinationPhoto.h}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h3>{T(node('vaccinations', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('vaccinations', 0)}</p>
    <p>
      The first vaccination, against distemper, parvovirus, leptospirosis and hepatitis. The
      microchip. Worming and a flea treatment. A veterinary examination. And the signed card
      recording all four, travelling with the puppy rather than following it on.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('vaccinations', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('vaccinations', 1)}</p>
    <p>
      The second dose and the yearly booster are your vet's, and so is anything optional.
      <a href="https://www.pdsa.org.uk/pet-help-and-advice/pet-health-hub/other-veterinary-advice/dog-vaccines" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the PDSA's guide to dog vaccinations</a> is
      the independent account of what a full course covers.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('vaccinations', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Required by law since 2016, done before the puppy leaves.
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/get-your-dog-cat-microchipped" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the government's guidance on the microchipping law</a> sets
      out the keeper's side of it, which becomes yours on the day you collect.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('vaccinations', 3).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Both are done here and dated on the card, so your vet picks the schedule up rather than
      starting it again.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('vaccinations', 4).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('vaccinations', 4)}</p>
    <p>
      <a href="/blue-staffy-pup-sale-uk/">See everything that comes home with a puppy</a> for the
      full list. No length of cover is stated on either page, because no file we keep holds one.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('A Card Is A Record, Not A Promise')}</h4>
    <p>
      Everything on it has already happened and can be checked with the practice that did it,
      which is exactly why it is worth more than a sentence on a website.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('The Chip Is The Legal Half')}</h5>
    <p>
      The vaccination is good practice; the chip is the law, and transferring the keeper details
      is the part new owners most often forget.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Health Note: Book the second dose before you collect')}</h6>
    <p>Your vet will want the card in front of them, and the gap between doses is theirs to set.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 07 registration — standard, NO PICK. Restored at Task 18b so the migrated registration H2
      and its "Commitment to Health" H3 have a section to stand in rather than being dropped
      (working rule 15). The H2 is the migrated one with "blue staffy puppy" narrowed to "Staffy
      puppy" (`verbatim.changed`). The InfoCard caveat beside the prose is this section's
      recorded refresh delta; the steel band the note also describes belongs to a `standard` S3
      pick, and this section carries no pick, so no box class is invented for it. */}
  <section id="registration" data-section-label="What KC registration covers">
    <h2>{T(heading('registration'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('registration')}</p>

    <h3>{T(node('registration', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('registration', 0)}</p>

    <h3>{T(node('registration', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Parentage, the litter, and the breeder who registered it — which is why you should be
      handed the original rather than a photocopy.
      <a href="/blue-staffy-uk-breeders/">See the parents each litter is registered to</a> for
      both dogs' pedigrees.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('registration', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      It is not a health test and it is not a quality mark. The DNA tests higher up this page
      are about health; registration is a paper trail about parentage, and the two are
      separate documents on purpose.
    </p>
    <InfoCard
      kind="observed"
      heading={T('A Badge Read As A Test')}
      body="Registration and health screening get quoted in the same breath more often than any other pair of claims on a breeder's website. They answer different questions, and only one of them has a result."
    />

    <h4>{T('You Can Check The Trail Without Us')}</h4>
    <p>
      Both parents' registration numbers are yours on request, and the registry, not this page,
      is where they are worth checking.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('Registered Breeders Tend To Be Reading The Guidance')}</h5>
    <p>
      That is the honest form of the claim in the paragraph above: a correlation worth something,
      and not a test result.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Health Note: Papers and results are two envelopes')}</h6>
    <p>Ask for both by name, because a breeder holding one is not necessarily holding the other.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 08 vet-checks — standard, NO PICK. The most useful thing on the page and the one none of
      the four scanned competitors carries: what an early warning actually looks like. The six
      signals are the migrated body's own list, stated as what to WATCH for and never as a
      diagnosis. The second H3 and its opening are migrated wording, including the reminder that
      this is not veterinary advice. Compact rows against the airier sections around it, which
      is the recorded refresh delta. */}
  <section id="vet-checks" data-section-label="Vet checks and warning signs">
    <h2>{T(heading('vet-checks'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('vet-checks')}</p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={vetCheck.file}
      alt={vetCheck.alt}
      width={vetCheck.w}
      height={vetCheck.h}
      {/* 1080px master against a 420px column: the 400 and 760 candidates are baked beside it
          for the reason above, and `sizes` says what the column paints. */}
      srcset={srcsetOf(vetCheck, [400, 760])}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h3>{T(node('vet-checks', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Within forty-eight to seventy-two hours of coming home, and before anything is wrong: it
      puts your own vet's eyes on the puppy at the start rather than in an emergency.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('vet-checks', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('vet-checks', 1)}</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Appetite or thirst changing from that puppy's normal.</li>
      <li>Tiredness that does not fit the day.</li>
      <li>Vomiting or diarrhoea that persists rather than passes.</li>
      <li>Limping, or favouring a leg.</li>
      <li>Itching hard enough to break the skin.</li>
      <li>Coughing, or nasal discharge.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>{T(node('vet-checks', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      We are breeders. Every line above is a reason to ring a vet and none is a diagnosis we can
      make, which is why this section names signals and stops.
      <a href="https://www.rspca.org.uk/adviceandwelfare/pets/dogs/puppy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the RSPCA's independent puppy advice</a> covers
      the same ground, written by somebody with nothing to sell you.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('The Baseline Is The Useful Part')}</h4>
    <p>
      Most of that list is a change rather than a symptom, so the first fortnight of learning
      what normal looks like for your dog is what makes any of it legible.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('Early Is Cheaper, In Both Senses')}</h5>
    <p>A phone call is free and an ignored fortnight rarely is, for you or for the puppy.</p>
    <h6>{T('Health Note: Tell us too')}</h6>
    <p>If something shows up in the first weeks we want to know, because the litter shares parents.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 09 diet — standard, NO PICK. What the litter is weaned onto and what to keep doing. NO
      BRAND AND NO QUANTITY: no file on disk holds either, and a gram figure invented here would
      be the kind of claim rule 9 forbids. The recorded refresh delta is brass as a hairline
      rather than as a fill, which is a stylesheet matter and not a class this section invents. */}
  <section id="diet" data-section-label="Diet and nutrition">
    <h2>{T(heading('diet'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('diet')}</p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={feeding.file}
      alt={feeding.alt}
      width={feeding.w}
      height={feeding.h}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h3>{T(node('diet', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      The litter is weaned onto a premium puppy food and you go home knowing which one. Change
      it if you want to; change it over a week or so rather than in a day, because the stomach
      is the part that notices.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('diet', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Set meals at set times rather than a bowl left down. A Staffy will eat what is in front of
      it, and weight is the single thing an owner controls that shortens this breed's life.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('diet', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>Clean water, available all the time, changed rather than topped up.</p>

    <h3>{T(node('diet', 3).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      The everyday human foods a dog should not have: chocolate, grapes and raisins, onions and
      garlic, and anything sweetened with xylitol.
      <a href="/blue-staffy-blog-guides/">Our blog guides go further on feeding a new puppy</a> for
      the first fortnight in detail, which is the fiddly part.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('No Brand And No Gram Figure Here')}</h4>
    <p>
      We hold no file stating a quantity for a given weight, so this page states none; the bag
      and your vet both do, and one of them knows your dog.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('The Ribs Are The Measure')}</h5>
    <p>Easy to feel and not to see is the whole of it, and it costs nothing to check weekly.</p>
    <h6>{T('Health Note: The skin often answers to the bowl')}</h6>
    <p>Where a Staffy is itching, the diet is worth a word with your vet before anything else.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 10 exercise — standard, NO PICK. One to two hours of daily activity for a grown dog, which
      is the migrated body's own figure and the only figure in this section; nothing beyond it
      is stated. The third H3 and its opening are migrated wording. The recorded refresh delta
      is a two-column ledger reading rather than the running prose around it. */}
  <section id="exercise" data-section-label="Exercise, mind and grooming">
    <h2>{T(heading('exercise'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('exercise')}</p>

    <h3>{T(node('exercise', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      One to two hours a day for a grown dog: brisk walking, running in a secure space, and
      play. A puppy does far less than that and builds up to it, because the joints are still
      arriving. The figure matters for this breed in particular — a Staffy with nothing to do
      finds something, and the something is usually a door frame.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('exercise', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Puzzle feeders, short training sessions, and play with a person in it. Ten minutes of
      thinking tires this breed more reliably than another mile does, and it is the half most
      often skipped.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('exercise', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('exercise', 2)}</p>

    <h4>{T('A Short Coat Is Not A No-Maintenance Coat')}</h4>
    <p>
      The brushing is quick and the skin underneath is the reason to do it: a blue Staffy shows
      irritation early, which is a gift if somebody is looking.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('Teeth Are The Part Everybody Puts Off')}</h5>
    <p>Dental disease is slow, ordinary and preventable, and it is worth a minute a day.</p>
    <h6>{T('Health Note: Two walks beat one long one')}</h6>
    <p>Split the hour and the dog settles better between them, which is what you are after.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 11 conditions — standard, NO PICK. The five the migrated body names and no others, each
      with one plain sentence saying what it is. NO PREVALENCE FIGURE, NO PERCENTAGE AND NO
      PROGNOSIS: the migrated body gives none and this page invents none. Four of the six H3s
      carry migrated wording and their own openings. The InfoCard caveat beside the list is this
      section's recorded refresh delta; the band the note also describes is a `standard` S3
      pick, and this section carries none. */}
  <section id="conditions" data-section-label="The conditions we watch for">
    <h2>{T(heading('conditions'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('conditions')}</p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={certifiedBreeders.file}
      alt={certifiedBreeders.alt}
      width={certifiedBreeders.w}
      height={certifiedBreeders.h}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h3>{T(node('conditions', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('conditions', 0)}</p>

    <h3>{T(node('conditions', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('conditions', 1)}</p>
    <p>The first of the two DNA tests in the table above.</p>

    <h3>{T(node('conditions', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('conditions', 2)}</p>
    <p>The second, recorded in the same table.</p>

    <h3>{T(node('conditions', 3).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      A kneecap that slips out of its groove. It is graded by a vet rather than screened by DNA,
      which is why it is in the screening section and not the table.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('conditions', 4).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('conditions', 4)}</p>

    <h3>{T(node('conditions', 5).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      A skin mite most dogs carry harmlessly, which can flare in a young or run-down dog — a
      veterinary diagnosis, and the last condition this page names.
      <a href="/uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide/">Our breed guide covers the build and temperament behind these</a> if
      you would rather read the breed described than its risks listed.
    </p>
    <InfoCard
      kind="observed"
      heading={T('Five Named, And No Numbers')}
      body="No percentage, prevalence or prognosis appears beside any of the five: no file we keep states one."
    />
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.thekennelclub.org.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Kennel Club's own health information for the breed</a> keeps
      the breed-wide picture, which is not ours to summarise.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('A List Is Not A Forecast')}</h4>
    <p>
      Naming what can go wrong is what separates a health page from a sales page, and only one
      of the four breeder pages we read does it at all. It describes a breed, not your dog.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('Two Of The Five Are Screened Out Before A Mating')}</h5>
    <p>The other three are managed rather than screened, which is why the care sections exist.</p>
    <h6>{T('Health Note: Bring this list to your first appointment')}</h6>
    <p>Your vet knows it already, and it makes the conversation about your dog faster.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 12 lifespan — standard, NO PICK. The question a family actually has behind every other
